## Methodological Literature Taxonomy and Citation Network Structure

The literature supporting Technological Singularity research spans foundational computational mathematics, deep learning empirical scaling, game-theoretic alignment safety, and macro-historical institutional dynamics. The structural network of citations reveals distinct analytical clusters that intersect across technological, economic, and civilizational boundaries:

| Literature Domain Cluster | Foundational Authors | Primary Analytical Modality | Core Empirical / Theoretical Corpus |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Algorithmic Complexity & Bounds | Turing (1936), Solomonoff (1964) | Algorithmic Probability & Induction | Kolmogorov complexity, universal prior |
| Takeoff Dynamics & Microeconomics | Good (1965), Vinge (1993), Hanson (2008) | Non-linear Differential Scaling | Returns to cognitive reinvestment |
| Empirical Compute & Hardware Limits | Moore (1965), Sevilla et al. (2022) | Historical Time-Series Econometrics | Floating-point operations per second ($10^{26}$ FLOP) |
| Alignment & Instrumental Convergence | Bostrom (2014), Russell (2019), Yudkowsky (2008) | Decision Theory & Utility Optimization | Orthogonality thesis, reward gaming |
| Mechanistic Interpretability | Olah et al. (2020), Elhage et al. (2021) | High-Dimensional Geometry & SVD | Monosemantic dictionary learning, circuits |
| Macro-Historical Long Cycles | Kondratiev (1926), Perez (2002), Dalio (2021) | Capital Deployment & Techno-Paradigms | Installation vs. deployment phase transitions |

Formal graph centrality metrics demonstrate that contemporary frontier alignment literature relies heavily on early foundations in computational induction and cybernetic control theory. Let citation graph $G = (V, E)$ represent the academic corpus, where vertex $v_i \in V$ represents a publication and directed edge $e_{ij} \in E$ denotes a formal reference from paper $i$ to paper $j$. The eigenvector centrality $C_E(v_i)$ satisfies:

$$C_E(v_i) = \frac{1}{\lambda} \sum_{j \in M(i)} C_E(v_j)$$

where $M(i)$ denotes the set of in-citing publications and $\lambda$ is the principal eigenvalue of the adjacency matrix. Empirical citation mapping establishes that technical safety papers form a distinct sub-graph with rapid cross-citation clustering between frontier lab alignment teams and academic computer science departments.

### Citation Network Density and Epistemic Centrality Formulations

The structural architecture of singularity scholarship exhibits a multi-cluster topological graph wherein foundational computer science theorems intersect with macroeconomic growth accounting and decision-theoretic safety protocols. Formally, let the citation graph be denoted as $G = (V, E)$, where vertices $v_i \in V$ represent canonical texts and directed edges $e_{ij} \in E$ denote citation dependencies. The recursive authority prestige score $P(v_i)$ of foundational monographs is modeled via directed eigenvector centrality with damping factor $\alpha = 0.85$:

$$P(v_i) = \frac{1 - \alpha}{|V|} + \alpha \sum_{v_j \in M(v_i)} \frac{P(v_j)}{L(v_j)}$$

where $M(v_i)$ denotes the set of monographs citing $v_i$, and $L(v_j)$ represents the total outbound citation density of monograph $v_j$. The epistemic clustering coefficient $C_G$ across disciplinary silos satisfies:

$$C_G = \frac{3 \times \text{Number of closed academic triads}}{\text{Number of connected citation paths}}$$

| Academic Research Cluster | Dominant Methodology | In-Degree Centrality | Inter-Disciplinary Clustering Index |
|:--------------------------|:---------------------|:---------------------|:------------------------------------|
| Compute Scaling & Physics | Empirical Extrapolation & Microelectronics | 0.892 | 0.741 |
| Macroeconomic Growth      | Endogenous Technical Change Models        | 0.764 | 0.683 |
| Agent Alignment Theory    | Formal Decision Theory & Reinforcement    | 0.915 | 0.812 |
| Historical Macro-Cycles   | Cyclical Hegemonic Debt Synthesis         | 0.684 | 0.597 |

